<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Zaporozhian Sich</h1><p class="st-meta">1552 CE – 1775 CE (approx.) · capital Khortytsia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The fortified island camp of the Zaporozhian Cossacks below the Dnieper rapids, a self-governing warrior republic and symbol of Ukrainian freedom.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From the 15th century runaway peasants, adventurers and townsmen gathered on the steppe frontier as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, fishing, hunting and fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>. Around 1552 Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmytro-vyshnevetsky">Dmytro Vyshnevetsky</a> built a fort on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mala-khortytsia">Mala Khortytsia</a>, often seen as the first Sich. Later Siches were built at several sites beyond the rapids, out of reach of royal officials.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Sich was a male military community governed by a general assembly, the rada, which elected the kish otaman and other officers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and lived in barracks by units called kurins. They raided Ottoman and Crimean coasts in light boats, served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> for pay and often rebelled against it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky's</a> uprising began there in 1648.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Peter I destroyed the Sich in 1709 because its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mazepa">Mazepa</a>. A new Sich was allowed in 1734, but in 1775 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II's</a> army surrounded and abolished it, exiling the last otaman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petro-kalnyshevsky">Petro Kalnyshevsky</a>. Its Cossacks dispersed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/danube-sich">Danube Sich</a> and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/black-sea-cossack-host">Black Sea Cossack Host</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zaporozhian-sich.jpg" alt="Zaporozhian Sich" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Zaporizhian_Sich.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1552 CE – 1775 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khortytsia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dmytro Vyshnevetsky, Ivan Sirko, Petro Kalnyshevsky</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zaporozhian Host, Sich</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1664&amp;focus=zaporozhian-sich" aria-label="Zaporozhian Sich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5498 503 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zaporozhian Sich lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5818 759L5457 693M5818 759L5754 721M5818 759L5820 533M5818 759L5808 852M5818 759L6487 457M5818 759L5641 873"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5457 693h0M5754 721h0M5820 533h0M5808 852h0M6487 457h0M5641 873h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5818 759h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5818 759h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Khortytsia. <a href="/atlas?year=1664&amp;focus=zaporozhian-sich">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
